## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Ollama/MiniCPM-V inference at 9,000 documents will be extremely time-consuming.** At ~10-30 seconds per image, this could take **25–75 hours** of continuous GPU inference. Each of the 9,000 specimens needs to be sent through the VLM via the Ollama API.

> [!WARNING]
> **Disk space**: 9,000 predictions + metrics JSONs + paired field observations CSV (9,000 × 20 fields = 180,000 rows) + statistical reports will consume significant disk space. The benchmark run artifacts will be stored under `backend/benchmark_reports/<runId>/` per project rules.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Is Ollama running with MiniCPM-V loaded?** The pipeline needs `http://localhost:11434` with `minicpm-v:latest` available. Please confirm Ollama is active before I start.

## Open Questions

1. **Inference Budget**: Given the ~25-75 hour runtime, should I:
   - **(a)** Run all 9,000 sequentially (most accurate, longest)?
   - **(b)** Run a representative sample (e.g., 100 per category = 900 total) first as a pilot, then scale up?
   - **(c)** Run with parallelism (multiple concurrent Ollama requests)?

2. **Which models to evaluate?** In the current paper (Table 8), we benchmark 4 systems:
   - Proposed AU DIC System (MiniCPM-V + 6-Stage Normalizer)
   - Pure Foundation VLM (MiniCPM-V raw)
   - Tesseract OCR
   - EasyOCR
   
   Should all 4 models run against all 9,000 documents? That would be **36,000 total inferences**.

3. **Dataset Selection Strategy**: The `D:\AU_DIC_Benchmark_60k` has 15,000 PDFs, 60,000 PNGs, 60,000 JPEGs, and 60,000 GTs. The GTs have 4 profiles per document (clean, scanner_copy, mobile_camera, rotated_90). I need to map your requested 9 categories to the correct files. Should I select 1,000 *unique* documents per category, or can the same document appear in multiple categories (different profiles)?

## Proposed Changes

### Phase 1: Dataset Selection & Staging Script
#### [NEW] [select_9k_benchmark_subset.py](file:///c:/github/academicuniverse/research/select_9k_benchmark_subset.py)
- Scans `D:\AU_DIC_Benchmark_60k` metadata/groundtruth files
- Selects 1,000 documents per each of the 9 categories (PDF-clean, PNG-clean, PNG-scanned, PNG-mobile, PNG-rotated, JPEG-clean, JPEG-scanned, JPEG-mobile, JPEG-rotated)
- Creates a staging manifest JSON mapping each specimen to its file path, GT path, and expected profile
- Validates that all 9,000 file paths exist before proceeding

---

### Phase 2: Live Inference Engine (9K Scale)
#### [NEW] [run_9k_benchmark.py](file:///c:/github/academicuniverse/research/run_9k_benchmark.py)
- Iterates through the 9,000-specimen manifest
- For each specimen: sends image/PDF to Ollama MiniCPM-V API → receives JSON prediction → applies 6-stage CanonicalNormalizer → compares against GT → classifies error type
- Saves per-document prediction JSON to `backend/benchmark_reports/<runId>/predictions/`
- Implements checkpoint/resume (saves progress every 100 documents so crashes don't lose work)
- Generates `paired_field_observations.csv` (180,000+ rows)
- Computes aggregate metrics per category and overall

---

### Phase 3: Statistical Analysis & Paper Tables
#### [NEW] [analyze_9k_results.py](file:///c:/github/academicuniverse/research/analyze_9k_results.py)
- Reads paired field observations CSV
- Computes per-category accuracy, CER, F1, precision, recall
- Runs statistical tests (McNemar, Wilcoxon, paired t-test, Bootstrap CIs)
- Generates publication-ready tables (LaTeX + Markdown)
- Produces accuracy breakdown by format × profile matrix

---

### Phase 4: Benchmark Report & Artifacts
All outputs stored in `backend/benchmark_reports/<runId>/`:
- `metrics.json` — Overall metrics
- `predictions.json` — Per-document predictions  
- `comparisons.json` — Cross-model comparison
- `paired_field_observations.csv` — 180K row statistical dataset
- `statistical_results.json` — Hypothesis test results
- `summary.md` — Human-readable report
- `execution.log` — Full execution log

